course: dictionary
lesson: 11998
title: Describing with characterise
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: When you characterise something, you say what it is typically like. Its main feature or quality is the thing that makes it what it is. «Deserts are characterised by little rain.» The main feature of a desert is that it gets very little rain.
explain: You can also use characterise to describe a person. «How would you characterise the main character?» This asks you to describe what the character is like, using clear words such as brave, kind or greedy.
explain: Look for the little words that come with it. Characterised by means having a certain feature as the main one. Characterise as means to describe someone or something as a certain kind of thing: «Critics characterised the film as funny and warm.»
fact: Characterise is a verb. Its other forms are characterises, characterised and characterising.
fact: «Characterise» means to be the typical feature of something, or to describe what someone or something is like.
fact: In American English the spelling is characterize, but the meaning is the same.
fact: Look for the phrase «characterised by» in science and English. It tells you the main feature of something.
fact: When you answer a question that says «characterise», you can describe the main features in your own words.
exercise: «Deserts are characterised by very little rain.» What does characterised by mean here? | *Have as their typical feature | Are found near the sea | Are cold at night only | Are sad places
exercise: Which words best characterise someone who always shares and is always kind? | Late for school | Tall and thin | Lost and tired | *Generous and kind
exercise: Which word means to describe what someone or something is like? | Stretch | Whistle | Forget | *Characterise
test: «Rainforests are characterised by heavy rain and tall trees.» What is a typical feature of a rainforest? | Sandy beaches | *Heavy rain and tall trees | Dry, empty plains | Snow all year round
test: «Could you characterise your favourite book in three words?» What is the teacher asking you to do? | *Describe what the book is like | Count the pages in the book | Read the book out loud | Copy the title onto the board
exam: In a story, «The fox was sly and always tricked the others», which word would best characterise the fox? | *Sly | Silent | Nervous | Gentle
